-- Passage rg3-connections-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('dc72dfbf-eabc-5870-86cc-3eb8ca727aa9', 'rg3-connections-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-connections'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Why Runners Warm Up$q$, $q$Before a race, you might see runners jogging slowly, swinging their arms and stretching their legs. They are not wasting time. They are warming up, and a warm up helps their bodies get ready.

When you sit still, your heart beats slowly. Your muscles are cool and a little stiff. If you start running fast right away, your body has to change too quickly. Stiff muscles can pull or strain, and that can hurt.

A good warm up starts with easy movement. Runners might jog or march in place for five minutes. This makes the heart beat faster. A faster heartbeat sends more blood to the muscles. Moving muscles also make heat, so they get warmer. Warm muscles stretch and bend more easily than cold ones.

After the easy jog, many runners do moving stretches. They might swing each leg forward and back or take long steps called lunges. These stretches help the joints move freely.

At the end of the race, runners do not just stop and sit down. They walk slowly for a few minutes. This is called a cool down. It lets the heart slow down a little at a time.

Coach Amara Osei tells the track team, "Warm up first, and your legs will thank you."$q$, 'draft')
