values ('9c352c05-f38f-5ce6-ac75-20b18bf24602', 'rg3-text-structure-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-text-structure'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Frogs and Toads: Alike and Different$q$, $q$Frogs and toads are both amphibians. That means they can live in water and on land. People often mix them up, but there are ways to tell them apart.

Heading: How They Are Alike

Both frogs and toads hatch from eggs laid in water. Both start life as tadpoles with tails. Both grow legs and lose their tails as they get older. Both catch insects with long, sticky tongues.

Heading: How They Are Different

A frog has smooth, wet skin. A toad, however, has dry, bumpy skin. Frogs have long back legs, so they can take big jumps. Toads have shorter legs, so they usually take small hops or walk. Frogs tend to stay close to ponds and streams. In contrast, toads can live farther from water, even in gardens.

Chart in words, title "Quick Check":
Skin: frog, smooth and wet. Toad, dry and bumpy.
Back legs: frog, long. Toad, short.
Home: frog, near water. Toad, can be far from water.

Key word box: Amphibian means an animal that lives part of its life in water and part on land.$q$, 'draft')
