insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('f0b92e42-b46a-531c-be39-f19d95fe2a99', 'rg3-text-structure-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-text-structure'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Why Leaves Change Color$q$, $q$In the fall, many trees turn red, orange and yellow. Have you ever wondered why this happens?

Heading: Green All Summer

In spring and summer, leaves are full of a green material called chlorophyll. Leaves use chlorophyll and sunlight to make food for the tree. Because there is so much chlorophyll, the leaves look green.

Heading: Shorter Days

In the fall, the days get shorter and the nights get cooler. As a result, trees stop making as much food. The chlorophyll in the leaves begins to break down. When the green fades, other colors that were hiding in the leaf can finally show. That is why we see yellow and orange.

Heading: Bright Reds

Some trees, such as maples, make a new red color in the fall. Sunny days and cool nights cause even more red to form. So a fall with lots of sunshine often has the brightest red trees.

Heading: Falling Down

Soon, a thin layer grows where each leaf meets its branch. This layer cuts the leaf off from the tree. Because of this, the leaf drops to the ground.

Picture caption: Red, yellow and orange leaves cover a park path in late October.$q$, 'draft')
