-- Passage rg3-text-structure-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d383230f-6d7d-5ab3-9fe3-fa293a2a1a80', 'rg3-text-structure-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g3-text-structure'), null,
        '3-5', $q$What Happens When Earthworms Dig$q$, $q$Earthworms are long, soft animals that live in the soil. They have no legs and no eyes. Even so, these small diggers can change a whole garden.

Heading: Digging Tunnels

Earthworms push and wiggle through the dirt. As they move, they eat bits of soil and dead leaves. They leave behind long, narrow tunnels.

Heading: Air and Water Get In

Because the soil is full of tunnels, it becomes loose instead of hard. As a result, air and rain can sink deep into the ground. The worms also leave castings in the soil. Castings are full of things that plants need to grow.

Heading: Help for Plants and Animals

The loose, rich soil affects many living things. Plant roots can spread out and soak up water more easily. So the plants grow taller and stronger. Since there are more plants, rabbits and insects find more food to eat. Birds such as robins also come to the garden to hunt for worms.

Sidebar, Night Crawlers: Many earthworms come up to the top of the soil at night, when the air is cool and damp.

Key word box: Castings means the waste that earthworms leave in the soil.$q$, 'draft')
