-- Passage rg4-authors-purpose-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('2cebf7a6-3533-5e9b-847f-5c1d213c3650', 'rg4-authors-purpose-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-authors-purpose'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The First Train Comes to Willow Creek$q$, $q$Account 1: A Letter Written by Clara Lindqvist in 1878

Dear Cousin Elsie,

Yesterday the first train rolled into Willow Creek! Papa closed the store so our whole family could go to the new station. We heard the whistle long before we saw the train. Then a cloud of black smoke appeared over the hill. The engine was louder than a thunderstorm, and the ground shook under my boots. Little Oskar covered his ears and hid behind Mama. When the train stopped, the band played and everyone cheered. Papa says the train will bring new goods for the store every week. I hope it brings you here for a visit soon!

Your cousin, Clara

Account 2: From a Book Called Our Prairie Towns, Written in 1990

In 1878, a railroad line reached the small farm town of Willow Creek. Before the railroad, supplies came by wagon. A trip to the nearest city took four days. The train cut that trip to just six hours. Farmers could now ship their wheat to big cities to sell. Within ten years, the town grew from about 300 people to more than 1,200. Some families who had lived there a long time missed the quiet days. Still, most historians agree that the railroad helped Willow Creek grow.$q$, 'draft')
