-- Passage rg4-character-setting-plot-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('941ad5a5-a807-5ebb-8600-3c2c99ecfdf6', 'rg4-character-setting-plot-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '3-5', $q$The One-Key Game$q$, $q$Nadia Hassan had been in the after-school coding club for six weeks. She could make a cartoon cat walk, jump and meow on the screen. For her final project, she wanted to make a game for her little brother, Omar, who was five.

In her first version, a cat chased falling fish. The player used four arrow keys and the space bar. Nadia thought it was perfect. She could score fifty points in one minute.

At home, she set the laptop in front of Omar. He pressed all the keys at once. The cat jumped off the screen, and the fish piled up at the bottom. Omar scored zero points. He pushed the laptop away and went to play with his blocks.

Nadia felt annoyed at first. Then she watched Omar stack his blocks. He used both hands and smiled every time a tower stood. He liked things that were simple.

The next day at club, Nadia changed her game. Now the fish fell slowly, and the player pressed only one big key to make the cat catch them. She added a happy sound every time a fish was caught.

That weekend, Omar played the new game for twenty minutes. He laughed each time the happy sound played. "Again!" he kept saying. The game was much too easy for Nadia now, and that was exactly right.$q$, 'draft')
